 "blockers": [
  {
   "where": "Acceptance Criteria KRN-LOAD-SEQ (L1093) and CR-1a step 6 (L60) vs CR-10 catch-up delivery (L131) and CR-15",
   "issue": "The ADR-0001 sync added KRN-LOAD-SEQ and `deliver_frame_end()` without adding the rules they test. KRN-LOAD-SEQ asserts that `PresentationClock.load_seq` increases by 1 on `LOADING -> RUNNING` (restore and new game), not on `PAUSED` or `SAVING -> RUNNING`, and that it moves before the `deliver_all` batches. Neither `PresentationClock`, `load_seq` nor `deliver_all` is defined anywhere in the GDD body; they exist only in ADR-0001 and ADR-0004. `deliver_frame_end()` is also never defined in the GDD, which only says it is the last driver step. CR-10 still says the merged PRESENTATION/PROJECTOR/DIAGNOSTIC batch is delivered 'once, after the frame's last tick' and 'at the exit' of the loop. An implementer reading only this GDD cannot tell that the merged delivery, the deferred subscription application and the post-restore `ALL` batch belong to `deliver_frame_end` and `deliver_all`, which is after forgiveness, the result flush and the paused service. The AC [CR-1a, CR-15] therefore points at rules that do not state it. The Pixel-Perfect GDD relies on this contract for its restore CUT.",
   "fix": "Add one paragraph to CR-10 or CR-15 and cross-reference it from CR-1a step 6, stating the following.\n1. `deliver_frame_end()` applies deferred subscription changes, then delivers each dirty frame buffer to `FRAME_MERGED` classes (ADR-0004 s5). It runs once on every exit path.\n2. On `LOADING -> RUNNING` the kernel increments the read-only `PresentationClock.load_seq`, then calls `deliver_all` (one `ALL` batch per block to every class, tick = restored T, with `snap_baseline` on entry to RUNNING). `load_seq` is never incremented on `PAUSED` or `SAVING -> RUNNING`.\n3. The merged-batch wording 'after the frame's last tick' means at `deliver_frame_end`, not at loop exit.\nAs an alternative, re-tag KRN-LOAD-SEQ as owned by ADR-0001 and ADR-0004, but only if the GDD states that ownership."
